{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8424be4bfdd34667","type":"tool_result","content":"14:**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of ModelRegistry, Catalog, and AIHub custom resources, provisioning model-registry service instances with kube-rbac-proxy authentication, PostgreSQL storage, and Gateway API or OpenShift Route ingress. [source: cmd/modelregistry.go:169, internal/controller/modelregistry_controller.go:282, internal/controller/modelcatalog_controller.go:183]\n16:**Detailed**: The model-registry-operator is a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles three CRDs — ModelRegistry (v1alpha1/v1beta1), Catalog (v1alpha1), and AIHub (v1alpha1) — to provision and manage model registry service instances within the RHOAI platform. For each ModelRegistry CR, the operator creates a Deployment running the model-registry proxy backed by either PostgreSQL or MySQL storage, with an optional kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that enforces SubjectAccessReview-based authorization on the HTTPS port. The operator manages the full resource lifecycle including Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC Roles/Bindings, Secrets, NetworkPolicies, and ingress resources (Gateway API HTTPRoutes or OpenShift Routes depending on cluster capabilities). The ModelCatalog reconciler similarly provisions a centralized catalog service with its own PostgreSQL instance. The operator detects cluster capabilities at startup — including OpenShift APIs, BYOIDC mode (where user.openshift.io is absent), Gateway API availability, and the platform Auth CRD — to conditionally create platform-specific resources. [source: internal/controller/util.go:36-39, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:197-211, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\n53:| model-catalog-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgresql ({image}) |\n57:| {registry-name}-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgres ({image}) |\n137:| model-catalog-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n141:| {registry-name}-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n246:| model-catalog-postgres | Opaque | model-catalog/catalog, model-catalog-postgres/postgresql | controller template | Unknown |\n248:| {registry-name}-postgres-credentials | Opaque | {registry-name}-postgres/postgres | controller Go source | Unknown |\n250:| {name}-postgres | Opaque |  | controller Go source | Unknown |\n302:- **Data storage:** Each model-registry instance connects to a PostgreSQL or MySQL database with configurable connection parameters including SSL/TLS certificates, keys, and root CA bundles mounted from Secrets and ConfigMaps. Database credentials are injected via environment variables from referenced Secrets. [source: internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:40-68, 107-131]\n----\n----\nai4rag.md:16:**Detailed**: ai4rag provides an end-to-end RAG optimization toolkit that orchestrates document ingestion from S3-compatible storage, text chunking (via Docling and LangChain strategies), embedding generation, vector store indexing (Milvus, PGVector, or Chroma), retrieval, LLM-based answer generation, and evaluation. It uses a hyperparameter optimization loop based on Generalized Additive Models (GAM) to search over chunking parameters, embedding models, foundation models, and retrieval strategies, producing ranked RAG pattern configurations. The library connects to an OpenAI-compatible Model-as-a-Service (MaaS) endpoint for both LLM inference and embedding generation, and includes a KFP event handler for integration with Data Science Pipelines. It is packaged as a Python library with no HTTP server or Kubernetes deployment manifests of its own. [source: pyproject.toml:6-7, 30-44, ai4rag/components/utils/maas_client.py:30-62, ai4rag/utils/event_handler/event_handler.py:47-55, ai4rag/core/hpo/gam_opt.py:1]\nai4rag.md:20:ai4rag is structured as a layered Python library organized around a RAG pipeline lifecycle: data ingestion, chunking, embedding, vector store management, retrieval, LLM generation, and evaluation. The optimization layer wraps this pipeline in a hyperparameter search driven by a GAM-based optimizer that explores combinations of chunking strategies (Docling hierarchical, LangChain recursive), embedding models, foundation models, and retrieval parameters to produce ranked RAG pattern configurations. [source: ai4rag/components/optimization/rag_templates_optimization.py:1-46, ai4rag/core/hpo/gam_opt.py:1]\nai4rag.md:24:The vector store layer supports three pluggable backends — Milvus (with gRPC TLS and PEM certificate materialization), PGVector (PostgreSQL with connection pooling), and Chroma (ephemeral, persistent, or client/server modes) — all configured via environment variables. Credentials for S3 storage, vector stores, and the MaaS endpoint are injected through environment variables, with no Kubernetes Secret manifests defined by the component itself. [source: ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:1-258, ai4rag/components/utils/s3.py:10-31]\nai4rag.md:94:| pgvector | ~=0.5.0 | Yes | Unknown | Python package dependency |\nai4rag.md:150:| PGVECTOR_PASSWORD | environment variable | Python application | runtime environment | Unknown |\nai4rag.md:181:| **Certificate handling** | System trust store by default; PEM certificate injection for Milvus self-signed endpoints; verify=False fallback for MaaS | ai4rag/rag/vector_store/milvus.py:47-74, ai4rag/components/utils/maas_client.py:57-59 |\nai4rag.md:188:- **Embedding and indexing:** Chunked text is sent to an OpenAI-compatible MaaS endpoint for embedding generation, then indexed in the configured vector store (Milvus via gRPC with optional TLS, PGVector via PostgreSQL wire protocol, or Chroma via HTTP). [source: ai4rag/rag/embedding/openai_model.py:31-36, ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:108-177, 181-249]\nai4rag.md:189:- **RAG query and evaluation:** Retrieval queries embed the question via the same MaaS endpoint, retrieve relevant chunks from the vector store, construct a prompt with context, and send it to the foundation model endpoint for LLM chat completion. Evaluation metrics (faithfulness, answer correctness, context correctness) are computed to score each RAG configuration. [source: ai4rag/rag/foundation_models/openai_model.py:25-96, ai4rag/components/optimization/rag_templates_optimization.py:39-46]\nai4rag.md:190:- **Security context:** 3 secret references (AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, MILVUS_TOKEN, PGVECTOR_PASSWORD) provide credential inputs for external service connections. All credentials are sourced from environment variables; no Kubernetes Secret manifests are defined by the component. [source: ai4rag/components/utils/s3.py:10, ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:175, 248]\nai4rag.md:206:| ca822e8 | 2026-08-12 | fix(components): add vector_db_secret_name to the indexing pipeline params |\nai4rag.md:211:| 26ed171 | 2026-08-05 | Switch vector store to direct clients (#118) |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8ad4966f4dc767f","agent_id":"acde594ef08b59665","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"6ff35b54-1613-4bc7-aa9e-bc6816f53baf","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:43:09.099Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8424be4bfdd34667","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
