{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ee5b7bdb-a762-4f5d-a3da-4946d1d2d58d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa923f427b925c95","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-_gv0xqa4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio and Auto Rag)\n\nTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold and not docs-only. The strategy is a\nBlocker-priority, platform-level capability spanning architecturally distinct\nsub-systems (the OGX distribution's vector I/O provider layer vs. the Gen AI\nStudio provisioning layer in odh-dashboard), carries genuine unknowns (the\nRHAISTRAT-7164 go/no-go that decides which provisioning mechanism exists), and\nhas an external dependency with uncertain timing. Full decomposition follows.\n\n## Input Basis and Staff Engineer Overrides\n\nThe full strategy body exceeds Jira's description size limit and is stored as\na Jira attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in this\nworkspace. The decomposition is therefore built from the strategy summary,\nAcceptance Criteria, Open Questions, and — decisively — the Staff Engineer\nInput, which per the decomposition rules takes precedence over the AI-generated\nTechnical Approach. HLRs below are derived from the strategy's Acceptance\nCriteria and Open Questions. Logged Staff Engineer overrides:\n\n1. **OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL** — OGX defaults to SQLite for\n   application storage; introducing PostgreSQL for vector storage is new work,\n   not an extension of existing infrastructure.\n2. **No custom image build and no Konflux onboarding** — the Red Hat\n   `postgresql-16` image already includes pgvector, the model-registry-operator\n   already auto-provisions it, and the related image env var is already present\n   in the rhods-operator CSV. The strategy's 1-2 sprint image-onboarding\n   estimate is eliminated; no `repo-onboarding`, `konflux-onboarding`, or\n   `license-validation` epics are generated.\n3. **Platform-level shared database deferred to RHOAI 3.6** — for 3.5, Gen AI\n   Studio must provision its own PostgreSQL instance; portability to the future\n   shared database is a design constraint, not a deliverable.\n4. **Hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization)** —\n   operator-based self-provisioning is only possible once that lands; a go/no-go\n   gate on its delivery timing decides the provisioning mechanism.\n5. **Decouple from Auto Rag** — Auto Rag compatibility with pgvector moves to\n   RHAISTRAT-7824's scope. Auto Rag is removed from affected components,\n   acceptance criteria, and validation requirements here; the strategy-level AC\n   \"Auto Rag functions with the default vector store\" is descoped (HLR-7 below).\n6. **Performance is dev-experimentation scope** — no strict latency SLAs; the\n   benchmark verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude as the inline\n   Milvus baseline on typical playground document sets; flat scan (no index) is\n   sufficient for the default. Open questions about latency thresholds, corpus\n   size ranges, and index type selection are removed.\n7. **Vector storage and application storage are independent configurations** —\n   only the pgvector `vector_io` provider is configured; the application storage\n   backend stays on SQLite. SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concerns are removed.\n8. **Existing remote vector store implementation is reusable** — the majority of\n   effort is infrastructure/provisioning of the PostgreSQL/pgvector database,\n   not the provider integration.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n| Strategy term | Canonical component (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Llama Stack distribution / inline Milvus provider | OGX Core | The Red Hat OGX distribution (formerly llama-stack) — see `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`. \"LlamaStack\" in the canonical list refers to the upstream stack; the distribution surface this strategy changes is OGX Core. |\n| Gen AI Studio (playground, BFF, provisioning) | Gen AI Studio | The gen-ai BFF and playground UI live in odh-dashboard (`packages/gen-ai`); owned by the Gen AI Studio team (dashboard-crimson-scrum). |\n| Auto Rag | AutoRAG | Removed from scope per Staff Engineer override 5. |\n| rhods-operator | — (no epic) | No code changes: the pgvector-capable PostgreSQL image env var is already present in the CSV per Staff Engineer Correction 2. Treated as a passive component; validation is covered by the provisioning epics' acceptance criteria. |\n\n## Epic List\n\nMain plan:\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Decide the PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning mechanism for the Gen AI Studio default vector store (go/no-go) | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | OGX | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set default resource sizing | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n\nConditional branches (not standalone Jira issues; attached to the gating\nInvestigation as a plan document — exactly one branch is selected):\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Gate |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 | Operator-based self-provisioning of the default pgvector instance | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | E001 outcome A |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 | BFF-managed provisioning of the default pgvector instance | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | E001 outcome B |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for playground RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | E001 outcome C |\n\n`epic_count` counts main-plan epics only (3); including branch epics the\ndecompositions comprises 6 epic files.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent (\"E002 --> E003\" means E002 must\ncomplete before E003 starts).\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Investigation: provisioning go/no-go (P0)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 OGX pgvector vector_io provider (P0)\"]\n    E003[\"E003 pgvector benchmark + sizing (P1)\"]\n    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 operator-based provisioning (P0)\"]\n    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 BFF-managed provisioning (P0)\"]\n    BC[\"BRANCH-C-E004 BYO PostgreSQL (P0)\"]\n    E002 --> E003\n    E001 --> BA\n    E001 --> BB\n    E001 --> BC\n    E002 --> BA\n    E002 --> BB\n    E002 --> BC\n```\n\nBranch epics are mutually exclusive conditional alternatives; they are excluded\nfrom the main-plan critical path. Main-plan critical path: E002 → E003\n(length 2).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 ∥ E002, E001 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 | The go/no-go decision on the provisioning mechanism is independent of the OGX-side provider enablement and of the benchmark; neither builds on the other. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 | The benchmark exercises the pgvector vector_io provider configuration that E002 delivers; it cannot run against a build without it. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-{A,B,C}-E004 | Rule 3 | True gate: the Investigation's outcome determines which provisioning epic exists at all (gate_failure_impact `rewrite` on every branch — a different outcome rewrites the provisioning approach). Bounded outcomes (3) → conditional branches. |\n| E002 → BRANCH-{A,B,C}-E004 | Rules 12/13 | Every provisioning variant wires OGX instances (OGXServer CR / BFF-created configuration) against the remote pgvector provider surface that E002 enables in the distribution; the consumers build against that artifact. |\n| E003 ∥ BRANCH-{A,B,C}-E004 | Rule 14 | The benchmark uses a development-provisioned pgvector instance and does not gate the provisioning implementation; the model-registry-operator's resource requests/limits are the starting point for sizing, adjusted later if benchmarking warrants. |\n| No onboarding chain (repo/konflux/license) | Rule 20, modified by Staff Engineer Correction 2 | The pgvector-capable Red Hat PostgreSQL image already exists and is already referenced in the rhods-operator CSV; no new component is onboarded. |\n| No docs-authoring epic | Step 5 boundary rules | The strategy does not declare Documentation Support: Yes; the documentation in branch C is substantive configuration guidance folded into that branch's scope because it cannot be authored or validated separately from the configuration support itself. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nHLRs are derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (HLR-1..HLR-6) and\nOpen Questions (HLR-6), since the full strategy attachment with the formal HLR\ntable is unavailable. HLR-7 records the descoped Auto Rag criterion.\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without users manually provisioning or configuring external infrastructure | RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-C-E004 (whichever branch the go/no-go selects) | The provisioning mechanism is exactly what the go/no-go decides; operator-based and BFF-managed deliver out-of-the-box, bring-your-own is the deliberate fallback when neither is viable in the release. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 + the selected branch epic | Provider capability (E002) and end-to-end playground validation (branch epic acceptance criteria) together cover this. |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (not an inline provider) | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | The remote pgvector provider is a separate PostgreSQL process by construction; corroborated by the OGX distribution context (remote-capable providers shipped, inline providers stripped). |\n| HLR-4 | P1 | The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 provisioning work: per-namespace scoping is a property of the same provisioning surface (namespace-scoped instance creation and wiring); a split would double-touch the same provisioning code and build the same surface twice. Per-namespace is the resolved direction (the per-instance consumer, Auto Rag, is descoped). |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | The default vector store is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead | RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | The benchmark/sizing epic's own deliverable; the selected branch epic applies the resulting requests/limits as an acceptance criterion. |\n| HLR-6 | P2 | Users have a migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: the migration path is documentation of the same provider-configuration surface E002 implements (pointing vector_io at an external database); a split would double-touch the same config surface. Resolved from an open question to an acceptance criterion because the answer does not change which epics exist. |\n| HLR-7 | P0 | Auto Rag functions with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | (descoped) | Removed per Staff Engineer override 5: Auto Rag compatibility with pgvector is addressed in RHAISTRAT-7824's scope (targeting RHOAI 3.6). Recorded here so the strategy-level criterion is visibly accounted for, not silently dropped. |\n\nOpen-question disposition (Step 3 decision rule):\n\n- **Scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance)**: resolved by Staff\n  Engineer input — per-namespace is preferred, and the only per-instance\n  consumer (Auto Rag) is descoped. Not an Investigation; an acceptance\n  criterion on the branch epics.\n- **Migration path to a production-grade vector DB**: the answer does not\n  change which epics exist (the provider configuration is swappable by\n  design). Acceptance criterion on E002.\n- **Provisioning mechanism / RHAISTRAT-7164 timing**: the answer changes which\n  implementation epics exist → Investigation E001 with three bounded outcomes\n  (conditional branches A/B/C).\n- **Latency thresholds, corpus size, index type**: removed per Staff Engineer\n  override 6 (dev-experimentation scope; order-of-magnitude check only).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the Affected Components table, Impacted Teams\n  table, formal HLR list, Dependencies table, and NFRs live in the Jira\n  attachment `RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`, which is not present in this\n  workspace. Team assignments (\"Gen AI Studio\", \"OGX\") are inferred from the\n  strategy labels (dashboard-crimson-scrum, playground) and the Staff Engineer\n  Input, and HLRs are derived from the Acceptance Criteria. Verify against the\n  attachment before committing release content.\n- **Cross-strategy hard dependency (schedule risk)**: operator-based\n  self-provisioning (branch A) is impossible until RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard\n  operator modularization, In Progress) delivers. The strategy cannot be\n  committed to a specific release until the go/no-go gate (E001) runs. The\n  branch structure plus the two fallbacks (BFF-managed, bring-your-own) bound\n  the blast radius; RHAISTRAT-7474 (inline provider removal) remains blocked on\n  whichever branch delivers.\n- **Priority inversion watch (non-blocking)**: the benchmark epic is P1 even\n  though the strategy is a Blocker. It verifies rather than enables — the\n  pgvector choice is already directed by Staff Engineer input, and no epic\n  depen