y which RHAISTRAT-7164's delivery status selects (or rules out) operator-based self-provisioning for the target release.\n- Analyze the lifecycle ownership of each option: creation, upgrade, cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart recovery, and credential rotation. For the BFF-managed option this analysis is mandatory — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup, restart handling, and credential rotation need explicit design answers before that option can be selected.\n- Analyze portability: the selected mechanism must be designed so that, when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), Gen AI Studio consumes a shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n- Confirm the resource-sizing starting point (the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits) applies to the selected mechanism.\n- Record the decision and rationale in a decision record that the downstream provisioning implementation can be planned against.\n\nOut of scope: implementing any provisioning mechanism; the OGX-side pgvector provider configuration; benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A decision record exists that selects exactly one of: operator-based self-provisioning, BFF-managed provisioning, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL for the default vector store in the target release.\n- The decision record states the go/no-go criteria and decision point against RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing, so the gate can be re-run mechanically in sprint planning.\n- The decision record documents lifecycle ownership (creation, upgrade, cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart recovery, credential rotation) for the selected mechanism, including how the BFF-managed option addresses each lifecycle concern if selected.\n- The decision record documents how the selected mechanism remains portable to the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL (consuming a connection string rather than self-provisioning).\n- The decision record identifies which downstream provisioning work the selection instantiates, in sufficient detail for that work to be scheduled.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — a default vector store available out-of-the-box: this investigation determines which mechanism delivers (or explicitly defers, in the bring-your-own outcome) that requirement, and is the prerequisite for all provisioning implementation work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":83636,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8ad4966f4dc767f","agent_id":"acde594ef08b59665","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"b417cca2-22bd-490d-a99a-a1f86fa4390b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:49:48.665Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
