{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-20d445ee-4e10-4c59-b60f-dc713267a3f9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a433bb9b4a4ca71d","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-_gv0xqa4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E002.md","content":"# Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the vector-store capability itself: the OGX distribution (the Red Hat build of the AI agent gateway formerly known as llama-stack) is configured so that vector I/O for RAG is served by a remote pgvector provider — a PostgreSQL database with the pgvector extension — running as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server. Per the Staff Engineer Input, the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable; the work here is provider enablement and configuration in the distribution, not new provider code.\n\nTwo Staff Engineer corrections define the boundary of this work precisely:\n\n- Vector storage and application storage are independent configurations. This epic configures only the vector I/O provider (the remote pgvector provider, activated via its environment guard such as `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`); the application storage backend (SQLite key-value and SQL defaults) is deliberately untouched, and there is no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern.\n- No custom image build or pipeline onboarding is needed — the Red Hat `postgresql-16` image already ships pgvector. This epic consumes that image; it does not build one.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Activate the remote pgvector vector I/O provider in the OGX distribution configuration, guarded by its environment variable (the `remote::pgvector` selection), so deployments that enable it route vector operations to an external PostgreSQL+pgvector instance instead of any inline provider.\n- Reuse the existing remote vector store implementation in the distribution for the pgvector integration; write new provider code only where the existing implementation genuinely does not suffice.\n- Keep the application storage backend configuration unchanged (SQLite defaults); ensure vector-store configuration remains an independent axis from application storage configuration.\n- Support wiring the provider's connection details (endpoint, credentials) via the distribution's existing secret-handling mechanisms (environment variables and `_FILE`-suffixed secret resolution).\n- Document the configuration path for pointing the vector I/O provider at an external, production-grade vector database — the migration path from the default store for users whose workloads outgrow it.\n\nOut of scope: provisioning the PostgreSQL instance itself (that is the downstream provisioning work selected by the go/no-go decision); benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824); any change to application storage.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- An OGX distribution deployment with the pgvector provider enabled serves vector insert, search, and retrieval operations over its vector I/O API against a remote PostgreSQL+pgvector instance, with the vector store running as a separate process from the OGX server (no inline provider in the serving path).\n- The provider is activated by its documented environment guard, and the guard defaults leave non-RAG deployments unaffected.\n- The application storage backend configuration is unchanged from its current defaults, and vector-store configuration can be set independently of it.\n- Connection credentials are injectable via Kubernetes secrets using the distribution's existing secret-resolution mechanism, without exposing secrets in process arguments.\n- A documented configuration path exists for switching the vector I/O provider to an external production-grade vector database (the user migration path off the default store).\n- The playground's RAG vector operations work against the pgvector-backed provider with no loss of the current inline capability at the provider level.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-2 (P0, provider-level share)** — Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability: this epic provides the provider capability; end-to-end playground validation is completed by the selected provisioning epic's acceptance criteria.\n- **HLR-3 (P0)** — the default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance: delivered by the remote pgvector provider configuration.\n- **HLR-6 (P2, bundled)** — migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database: bundled here because it is documentation of the same provider-configuration surface this epic implements; splitting it out would double-touch the same configuration.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":84846,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8ad4966f4dc767f","agent_id":"acde594ef08b59665","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"618060cd-18a4-48a5-b55a-653888e7ece9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:50:08.987Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
