{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-51907e78-6783-4795-8f56-b359255fa4cb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7c20c4198a48bac","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-_gv0xqa4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004.md","content":"# Operator-based self-provisioning of the default pgvector instance\n\n> Conditional branch A — instantiated only if the provisioning go/no-go decision selects operator-based self-provisioning (the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164, delivers a RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time for the target release).\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic implements out-of-the-box provisioning of the default vector store using the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164). Self-provisioning PostgreSQL for Gen AI Studio following the model-registry-operator pattern requires a RHOAI-controlled operator; the upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic. When a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG is configured, the operator provisions a PostgreSQL instance with pgvector (using the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image, which includes pgvector out of the box) and wires the OGX instance to use it as its vector I/O provider.\n\nThe implementation must remain portable to the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL planned for RHOAI 3.6: the consuming configuration is a connection string, so the shared database can replace self-provisioning without redesign.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization: provisioning the database Deployment, Service, persistent storage, and credentials Secret per namespace, following the model-registry-operator's auto-provisioning pattern as the reference implementation.\n- Wire the provisioned instance into the OGX server configuration for playgrounds with RAG, activating the remote pgvector vector I/O provider with the provisioned connection details.\n- Scope instances per namespace for multi-tenant isolation (per-namespace is the resolved scoping direction; the per-instance consumer, Auto Rag, is handled in RHAISTRAT-7824).\n- Apply resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing, adjusted per the benchmark recommendation where available.\n- Design for portability: the OGX configuration consumes a connection string, so a platform-provided shared database (RHOAI 3.6) can replace self-provisioning without changes to the consuming side.\n- Handle lifecycle end-to-end in the operator: creation, upgrade, credential management, and cleanup when the owning playground (or its namespace resources) is deleted.\n\nOut of scope: the OGX-side provider enablement (delivered by the distribution's remote pgvector provider configuration); benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824); the platform-shared database itself (RHOAI 3.6).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A user who creates a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG capabilities gets a working default vector store with no manual provisioning or external infrastructure configuration (out-of-the-box experience preserved).\n- The PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (Deployment, Service, persistent storage, credentials Secret) is provisioned and managed by the operator, and is cleaned up when the owning playground is deleted.\n- Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function end-to-end against the default vector store with no loss of current capability (document ingestion, retrieval, and query flows).\n- Vector store instances are isolated per namespace; one namespace's playground cannot read another namespace's vector data through the default store.\n- The provisioned instance's resource requests/limits follow the agreed sizing (model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per benchmark where applicable) and run without significant overhead in resource-constrained environments.\n- The OGX-side wiring consumes a connection-string style configuration, so a platform-provided shared PostgreSQL can replace the self-provisioned instance without changes to the consuming configuration.\n- The pgvector-capable PostgreSQL image reference used for provisioning is resolvable in disconnected installs (the related image env var already present in the rhods-operator CSV).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning: delivered by operator-based self-provisioning.\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability: end-to-end validation acceptance criteria above.\n- **HLR-4 (P1, bundled)** — multi-tenant scoping (per-namespace isolation): a property of the same provisioning surface; bundled rather than split.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":86625,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8ad4966f4dc767f","agent_id":"acde594ef08b59665","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"09c2b4c1-c1af-4e0d-9b36-9e894e254d3b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:50:40.674Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
