{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-51907e78-6783-4795-8f56-b359255fa4cb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8951d98910d60b9b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-_gv0xqa4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-managed provisioning of the default pgvector instance\n\n> Conditional branch B — instantiated only if the provisioning go/no-go decision selects BFF-managed provisioning (the dashboard operator modularization does not land in time, and the team opts to preserve the out-of-the-box experience rather than requiring bring-your-own PostgreSQL).\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic implements out-of-the-box provisioning of the default vector store directly in the Gen AI Studio backend-for-frontend (the gen-ai BFF), as the alternative to an operator-based approach when the RHOAI-controlled operator layer is not available in the target release. Following the pattern the BFF already uses for guardrails infrastructure, the BFF creates the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance — Deployment, Service, persistent storage (PVC), and credentials Secret — when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, using the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image (pgvector included). The OGX instance is then wired to use it as its vector I/O provider.\n\nThe Staff Engineer Input flags the caveats that shape this epic: the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation need explicit design attention; and the implementation must be designed for portability, so that when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6) the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement provisioning logic in the gen-ai BFF: when a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the pgvector-capable PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and credentials Secret; reuse the existing instance when one is already present.\n- Wire the provisioned instance into the OGX server configuration for the playground, activating the remote pgvector vector I/O provider with the provisioned connection details.\n- Implement lifecycle handling explicitly (no reconciliation loop): cleanup of the database resources when the owning playground is deleted; recovery from BFF pod restarts (idempotent re-check of existing resources); a defined approach to credential rotation.\n- Scope instances per namespace for multi-tenant isolation (per-namespace is the resolved scoping direction; the per-instance consumer, Auto Rag, is handled in RHAISTRAT-7824).\n- Apply resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing, adjusted per the benchmark recommendation where available.\n- Design for portability: prefer consuming an existing connection string when one is provided (the RHOAI 3.6 platform-shared database path) over always self-provisioning.\n\nOut of scope: the OGX-side provider enablement (delivered by the distribution's remote pgvector provider configuration); benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824); operator-based provisioning.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A user who creates a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG capabilities gets a working default vector store with no manual provisioning or external infrastructure configuration (out-of-the-box experience preserved).\n- The PostgreSQL/pgvector Deployment, Service, PVC, and credentials Secret are created by the BFF only when absent in the namespace, and an existing suitable instance is reused rather than duplicated.\n- Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function end-to-end against the default vector store with no loss of current capability (document ingestion, retrieval, and query flows).\n- Deleting a playground cleans up the database resources it caused to be provisioned (or documents and implements the deliberate retention policy decided in design).\n- A BFF pod restart does not leave orphaned or broken database resources: re-running the provisioning path is idempotent.\n- Credential rotation is handled per the designed approach without breaking existing playground RAG workflows.\n- Vector store instances are isolated per namespace; one namespace's playground cannot read another namespace's vector data through the default store.\n- The provisioned instance's resource requests/limits follow the agreed sizing (model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per benchmark where applicable) and run without significant overhead in resource-constrained environments.\n- The BFF consumes a provided shared connection string in preference to self-provisioning when one is available, demonstrating the RHOAI 3.6 portability path.\n- The pgvector-capable PostgreSQL image reference used for provisioning is resolvable in disconnected installs (the related image env var already present in the rhods-operator CSV).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0)** — default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning: delivered by BFF-managed provisioning.\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability: end-to-end validation acceptance criteria above.\n- **HLR-4 (P1, bundled)** — multi-tenant scoping (per-namespace isolation): a property of the same provisioning surface; bundled rather than split.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":86625,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8ad4966f4dc767f","agent_id":"acde594ef08b59665","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"4505ffa1-c0ff-44e5-aba1-44619792d664","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:50:57.080Z","subagent_type":"general-purpo