{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-51907e78-6783-4795-8f56-b359255fa4cb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-adb5f5454d73256f","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-_gv0xqa4/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004.md","content":"# Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for playground RAG\n\n> Conditional branch C — instantiated only if the provisioning go/no-go decision selects bring-your-own PostgreSQL (neither operator-based nor BFF-managed provisioning is viable for the target release).\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic implements the deliberate fallback in which customers provide their own PostgreSQL for RAG capabilities in the Gen AI Studio playground: users (or administrators) configure the OGXServer custom resource spec with their PostgreSQL connection details, and the playground's RAG workflows run against that database using the remote pgvector vector I/O provider. The Staff Engineer Input notes this aligns with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0, even though the playground largely abstracts OGX from users — so this epic also carries the burden of making the configuration path as discoverable and low-friction as the playground permits.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Support and validate the configuration path by which a user-supplied PostgreSQL (with pgvector) is attached to an OGX instance for playground RAG: OGXServer custom resource spec fields and the credentials Secret carrying connection details.\n- Surface the bring-your-own requirement in the Gen AI Studio playground where the abstraction would otherwise hide it: clear indication when no default vector store is available and RAG is requested, with guidance to the configuration path.\n- Document the setup end to end: preparing a PostgreSQL+pgvector instance, creating the credentials Secret, configuring the OGXServer spec, and verifying RAG works — including for disconnected installs where the user's PostgreSQL must be reachable from the cluster.\n- Validate that the documented path works with the remote pgvector vector I/O provider enabled in the OGX distribution.\n- Confirm per-namespace behavior: user-supplied instances and their credentials are referenced and scoped within the owning namespace.\n\nOut of scope: the OGX-side provider enablement (delivered by the distribution's remote pgvector provider configuration); provisioning a default instance (this branch exists precisely when that is not viable); benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A user-supplied PostgreSQL with pgvector, configured via the OGXServer spec and a credentials Secret, serves vector I/O for playground RAG workflows, verified end to end (document ingestion, retrieval, and query flows).\n- The playground clearly indicates when RAG is unavailable because no vector store is configured, and points to the documented configuration path.\n- The documented setup procedure is complete and has been validated on a clean environment, including the credentials Secret handling and connection details format.\n- Credentials are supplied via Secret references, never inline in the custom resource spec or exposed in process arguments.\n- User-supplied instances and credentials remain scoped to the owning namespace (no cross-namespace leakage through the playground path).\n- The documented configuration path remains the migration target for users outgrowing any future default store (consistent with the migration path off the default vector database).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0, fallback form)** — in this branch the out-of-the-box default is explicitly not delivered; the requirement is satisfied to the extent the Staff Engineer Input directs for this outcome (bring-your-own aligned with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes), and the strategy-level out-of-the-box criterion is recorded as consciously deferred.\n- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the user-provided vector store with no loss of current capability.\n- **HLR-4 (P1, bundled)** — multi-tenant scoping (per-namespace isolation of user-supplied instances): a property of the same configuration surface; bundled rather than split.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":86625,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8ad4966f4dc767f","agent_id":"acde594ef08b59665","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"337915d6-a3cb-4e6f-98c9-7c1215db0a31","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:51:12.099Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
