{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-baa3d881b9acc474","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio and Auto Rag)\n11\t\n12\tTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold and not docs-only. The strategy is a\n13\tBlocker-priority, platform-level capability spanning architecturally distinct\n14\tsub-systems (the OGX distribution's vector I/O provider layer vs. the Gen AI\n15\tStudio provisioning layer in odh-dashboard), carries genuine unknowns (the\n16\tRHAISTRAT-7164 go/no-go that decides which provisioning mechanism exists), and\n17\thas an external dependency with uncertain timing. Full decomposition follows.\n18\t\n19\t## Input Basis and Staff Engineer Overrides\n20\t\n21\tThe full strategy body exceeds Jira's description size limit and is stored as\n22\ta Jira attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in this\n23\tworkspace. The decomposition is therefore built from the strategy summary,\n24\tAcceptance Criteria, Open Questions, and — decisively — the Staff Engineer\n25\tInput, which per the decomposition rules takes precedence over the AI-generated\n26\tTechnical Approach. HLRs below are derived from the strategy's Acceptance\n27\tCriteria and Open Questions. Logged Staff Engineer overrides:\n28\t\n29\t1. **OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL** — OGX defaults to SQLite for\n30\t   application storage; introducing PostgreSQL for vector storage is new work,\n31\t   not an extension of existing infrastructure.\n32\t2. **No custom image build and no Konflux onboarding** — the Red Hat\n33\t   `postgresql-16` image already includes pgvector, the model-registry-operator\n34\t   already auto-provisions it, and the related image env var is already present\n35\t   in the rhods-operator CSV. The strategy's 1-2 sprint image-onboarding\n36\t   estimate is eliminated; no `repo-onboarding`, `konflux-onboarding`, or\n37\t   `license-validation` epics are generated.\n38\t3. **Platform-level shared database deferred to RHOAI 3.6** — for 3.5, Gen AI\n39\t   Studio must provision its own PostgreSQL instance; portability to the future\n40\t   shared database is a design constraint, not a deliverable.\n41\t4. **Hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization)** —\n42\t   operator-based self-provisioning is only possible once that lands; a go/no-go\n43\t   gate on its delivery timing decides the provisioning mechanism.\n44\t5. **Decouple from Auto Rag** — Auto Rag compatibility with pgvector moves to\n45\t   RHAISTRAT-7824's scope. Auto Rag is removed from affected components,\n46\t   acceptance criteria, and validation requirements here; the strategy-level AC\n47\t   \"Auto Rag functions with the default vector store\" is descoped (HLR-7 below).\n48\t6. **Performance is dev-experimentation scope** — no strict latency SLAs; the\n49\t   benchmark verifies pgvector is in the same order of magnitude as the inline\n50\t   Milvus baseline on typical playground document sets; flat scan (no index) is\n51\t   sufficient for the default. Open questions about latency thresholds, corpus\n52\t   size ranges, and index type selection are removed.\n53\t7. **Vector storage and application storage are independent configurations** —\n54\t   only the pgvector `vector_io` provider is configured; the application storage\n55\t   backend stays on SQLite. SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concerns are removed.\n56\t8. **Existing remote vector store implementation is reusable** — the majority of\n57\t   effort is infrastructure/provisioning of the PostgreSQL/pgvector database,\n58\t   not the provider integration.\n59\t\n60\t## Component Mapping Notes\n61\t\n62\t| Strategy term | Canonical component (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) | Note |\n63\t|---|---|---|\n64\t| Llama Stack distribution / inline Milvus provider | OGX Core | The Red Hat OGX distribution (formerly llama-stack) — see `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md`. \"LlamaStack\" in the canonical list refers to the upstream stack; the distribution surface this strategy changes is OGX Core. |\n65\t| Gen AI Studio (playground, BFF, provisioning) | Gen AI Studio | The gen-ai BFF and playground UI live in odh-dashboard (`packages/gen-ai`); owned by the Gen AI Studio team (dashboard-crimson-scrum). |\n66\t| Auto Rag | AutoRAG | Removed from scope per Staff Engineer override 5. |\n67\t| rhods-operator | — (no epic) | No code changes: the pgvector-capable PostgreSQL image env var is already present in the CSV per Staff Engineer Correction 2. Treated as a passive component; validation is covered by the provisioning epics' acceptance criteria. |\n68\t\n69\t## Epic List\n70\t\n71\tMain plan:\n72\t\n73\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n74\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n75\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Decide the PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning mechanism for the Gen AI Studio default vector store (go/no-go) | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n76\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | OGX | P0 |\n77\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and set default resource sizing | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n78\t\n79\tConditional branches (not standalone Jira issues; attached to the gating\n80\tInvestigation as a plan document — exactly one branch is selected):\n81\t\n82\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Gate |\n83\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|------|\n84\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 | Operator-based self-provisioning of the default pgvector instance | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | E001 outcome A |\n85\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 | BFF-managed provisioning of the default pgvector instance | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | E001 outcome B |\n86\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for playground RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 | E001 outcome C |\n87\t\n88\t`epic_count` counts main-plan epics only (3); including branch epics the\n89\tdecompositions comprises 6 epic files.\n90\t\n91\t## Dependency DAG\n92\t\n93\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (\"E002 --> E003\" means E002 must\n94\tcomplete before E003 starts).\n95\t\n96\t```mermaid\n97\tgraph TD\n98\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: provisioning go/no-go (P0)\"]\n99\t    E002[\"E002 OGX pgvector vector_io provider (P0)\"]\n100\t    E003[\"E003 pgvector benchmark + sizing (P1)\"]\n101\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E004 operator-based provisioning (P0)\"]\n102\t    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E004 BFF-managed provisioning (P0)\"]\n103\t    BC[\"BRANCH-C-E004 BYO PostgreSQL (P0)\"]\n104\t    E002 --> E003\n105\t    E001 --> BA\n106\t    E001 --> BB\n107\t    E001 --> BC\n108\t    E002 --> BA\n109\t    E002 --> BB\n110\t    E002 --> BC\n111\t```\n112\t\n113\tBranch epics are mutually exclusive conditional alternatives; they are excluded\n114\tfrom the main-plan critical path. Main-plan critical path: E002 → E003\n115\t(length 2).\n116\t\n117\t## DAG Justification\n118\t\n119\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n120\t|------|------|-----------|\n121\t| E001 ∥ E002, E001 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 | The go/no-go decision on the provisioning mechanism is independent of the OGX-side provider enablement and of the benchmark; neither builds on the other. |\n122\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 | The benchmark exercises the pgvector vector_io provider configuration that E002 delivers; it cannot run against a build without it. |\n123\t| E001 → BRANCH-{A,B,C}-E004 | Rule 3 | True gate: the Investigation's outcome determines which provisioning epic exists at all (gate_failure_impact `rewrite` on every branch — a different outcome rewrites the provisioning approach). Bounded outcomes (3) → conditional branches. |\n124\t| E002 → BRANCH-{A,B,C}-E004 | Rules 12/13 | Every provisioning variant wires OGX instances (OGXServer CR / BFF-created configuration) against the remote pgvector provider surface that E002 enables in the distribution; the consumers build against that artifact. |\n125\t| E003 ∥ BRANCH-{A,B,C}-E004 | Rule 14 | The benchmark uses a development-provisioned pgvector instance and does not gate the provisioning implementation; the model-registry-operator's resource requests/limits are the starting point for sizing, adjusted later if benchmarking warrants. |\n126\t| No onboarding chain (repo/konflux/license) | Rule 20, modified by Staff Engineer Correction 2 | The pgvector-capable Red Hat PostgreSQL image already exists and is already referenced in the rhods-operator CSV; no new component is onboarded. |\n127\t| No docs-authoring epic | Step 5 boundary rules | The strategy does not declare Documentation Support: Yes; the documentation in branch C is substantive configuration guidance folded into that branch's scope because it cannot be authored or validated separately from the configuration support itself. |\n128\t\n129\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n130\t\n131\tHLRs are derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (HLR-1..HLR-6) and\n132\tOpen Questions (HLR-6), since the full strategy attachment with the formal HLR\n133\ttable is unavailable. HLR-7 records the descoped Auto Rag criterion.\n134\t\n135\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n136\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n137\t| HLR-1 | P0 | A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI without users manually provisioning or configuring external infrastructure | RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-C-E004 (whichever branch the go/no-go selects) | The provisioning mechanism is exactly what the go/no-go decides; operator-based and BFF-managed deliver out-of-the-box, bring-your-own is the deliberate fallback when neither is viable in the release. |\n138\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 + the selected branch epic | Provider capability (E002) and end-to-end playground validation (branch epic acceptance criteria) together cover this. |\n139\t| HLR-3 | P0 | The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (not an inline provider) | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | The remote pgvector provider is a separate PostgreSQL process by construction; corroborated by the OGX distribution context (remote-capable providers shipped, inline providers stripped). |\n140\t| HLR-4 | P1 | The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 provisioning work: per-namespace scoping is a property of the same provisioning surface (namespace-scoped instance creation and wiring); a split would double-touch the same provisioning code and build the same surface twice. Per-namespace is the resolved direction (the per-instance consumer, Auto Rag, is descoped). |\n141\t| HLR-5 | P1 | The default vector store is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead | RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | The benchmark/sizing epic's own deliverable; the selected branch epic applies the resulting requests/limits as an acceptance criterion. |\n142\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Users have a migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: the migration path is documentation of the same provider-configuration surface E002 implements (pointing vector_io at an external database); a split would double-touch the same config surface. Resolved from an open question to an acceptance criterion because the answer does not change which epics exist. |\n143\t| HLR-7 | P0 | Auto Rag functions with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | (descoped) | Removed per Staff Engineer override 5: Auto Rag compatibility with pgvector is addressed in RHAISTRAT-7824's scope (targeting RHOAI 3.6). Recorded here so the strategy-level criterion is visibly accounted for, not silently dropped. |\n144\t\n145\tOpen-question disposition (Step 3 decision rule):\n146\t\n147\t- **Scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance)**: resolved by Staff\n148\t  Engineer input — per-namespace is preferred, and the only per-instance\n149\t  consumer (Auto Rag) is descoped. Not an Investigation; an acceptance\n150\t  criterion on the branch epics.\n151\t- **Migration path to a production-grade vector DB**: the answer does not\n152\t  change which epics exist (the provider configuration is swappable by\n153\t  design). Acceptance criterion on E002.\n154\t- **Provisioning mechanism / RHAISTRAT-7164 timing**: the answer changes which\n155\t  implementation epics exist → Investigation E001 with three bounded outcomes\n156\t  (conditional branches A/B/C).\n157\t- **Latency thresholds, corpus size, index type**: removed per Staff Engineer\n158\t  override 6 (dev-experimentation scope; order-of-magnitude check only).\n159\t\n160\t## Health Warnings\n161\t\n162\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the Affected Components table, Impacted Teams\n163\t  table, formal HLR list, Dependencies table, and NFRs live in the Jira\n164\t  attachment `RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`, which is not present in this\n165\t  workspace. Team assignments (\"Gen AI Studio\", \"OGX\") are inferred from the\n166\t  strategy labels (dashboard-crimson-scrum, playground) and the Staff Engineer\n167\t  Input, and HLRs are derived from the Acceptance Criteria. Verify against the\n168\t  attachment before committing release content.\n169\t- **Cross-strategy hard dependency (schedule risk)**: operator-based\n170\t  self-provisioning (branch A) is impossible until RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard\n171\t  operator modularization, In Progress) delivers. The strategy cannot be\n172\t  committed to a specific release until the go/no-go gate (E001) runs. The\n173\t  branch structure plus the two fallbacks (BFF-managed, bring-your-own) bound\n174\t  the blast radius; RHAISTRAT-7474 (inline provider removal) remains blocked on\n175\t  whichever branch delivers.\n176\t- **Priority inversion watch (non-blocking)**: the benchmark epic is P1 even\n177\t  though the strategy is a Blocker. It verifies rather than enables — the\n178\t  pgvector choice is already directed by Staff Engineer input, and no epic\n179\t  depends on its outcome. If the benchmark finds pgvector materially slower\n180\t  than the inline Milvus baseline (beyond an order of magnitude), escalate\n181\t  before release rather than treating it as a pass/fail gate.\n182\t- **Planning note (epic size, branch B)**: the BFF-managed provisioning epic\n183\t  may sit at the upper end of the ~2-week guideline because the lifecycle\n184\t  concerns the Staff Engineer flagged (cleanup on playground deletion,\n185\t  pod-restart handling, credential rotation) need explicit design attention on\n186\t  top of the provisioning itself. Kept as one logical change: the lifecycle\n187\t  handling is inseparable from the creation path it governs (same BFF code\n188\t  paths, same resources). **Re-split trigger**: split the lifecycle-management\n189\t  work out if the design finds it needs reconciliation machinery beyond plain\n190\t  create/update/delete calls in the BFF.\n191\t- **BFF is not an operator (branch B design risk)**: without a reconciliation\n192\t  loop, drift and partial failures are handled imperatively; the portability\n193\t  constraint to the RHOAI 3.6 platform-shared database must be designed in from\n194\t  the start (consume a connection string when one exists rather than always\n195\t  self-provisioning).\n196\t\n197\t## Tiered Delivery\n198\t\n199\tThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing) has uncertain timing,\n200\tand partial value is deliverable without it:\n201\t\n202\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E002 (remote pgvector provider\n203\t  in the OGX distribution) and E003 (benchmark + sizing) — provider capability\n204\t  and risk-reduction validation that hold regardless of the provisioning\n205\t  mechanism chosen.\n206\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: the selected branch epic (A, B, or C) — blocked by the\n207\t  E001 go/no-go, which itself tracks RHAISTRAT-7164's landing.\n208\t\n209\t## Conditional Branches\n210\t\n211\tE001 has three bounded outcomes; exactly one branch epic is instantiated:\n212\t\n213\t- **Branch A — operator-based self-provisioning**: RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in\n214\t  time. PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management is implemented in the\n215\t  RHOAI-controlled operator layer it delivers, following the\n216\t  model-registry-operator pattern.\n217\t- **Branch B — BFF-managed provisioning**: RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in\n218\t  time and the team opts to keep the out-of-the-box experience. The gen-ai BFF\n219\t  provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly (as it does guardrails\n220\t  infrastructure), with explicit lifecycle design.\n221\t- **Branch C — bring-your-own PostgreSQL**: neither of the above is viable for\n222\t  the release. Users configure the OGXServer CR spec with their PostgreSQL\n223\t  connection details; the playground abstracts this as far as practical.\n224\t\n225\tBranch epics set `branch`, `gated_by` (the Investigation), and\n226\t`gate_failure_impact` (`rewrite` — a different outcome rewrites the\n227\tprovisioning approach rather than merely trimming it). No main-plan epic lists\n228\ta branch epic in its dependencies.\n229\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84927dd4c0085fae","agent_id":"af44f3bd2f5ac49aa","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"497059fe-af96-4b2f-91eb-