{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-875ae26c89e8b519","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n3\ttitle: Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: OGX Core\n6\tteam: OGX\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 7\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Enable the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic delivers the vector-store capability itself: the OGX distribution (the Red Hat build of the AI agent gateway formerly known as llama-stack) is configured so that vector I/O for RAG is served by a remote pgvector provider — a PostgreSQL database with the pgvector extension — running as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server. Per the Staff Engineer Input, the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable; the work here is provider enablement and configuration in the distribution, not new provider code.\n34\t\n35\tTwo Staff Engineer corrections define the boundary of this work precisely:\n36\t\n37\t- Vector storage and application storage are independent configurations. This epic configures only the vector I/O provider (the remote pgvector provider, activated via its environment guard such as `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`); the application storage backend (SQLite key-value and SQL defaults) is deliberately untouched, and there is no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern.\n38\t- No custom image build or pipeline onboarding is needed — the Red Hat `postgresql-16` image already ships pgvector. This epic consumes that image; it does not build one.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Activate the remote pgvector vector I/O provider in the OGX distribution configuration, guarded by its environment variable (the `remote::pgvector` selection), so deployments that enable it route vector operations to an external PostgreSQL+pgvector instance instead of any inline provider.\n43\t- Reuse the existing remote vector store implementation in the distribution for the pgvector integration; write new provider code only where the existing implementation genuinely does not suffice.\n44\t- Keep the application storage backend configuration unchanged (SQLite defaults); ensure vector-store configuration remains an independent axis from application storage configuration.\n45\t- Support wiring the provider's connection details (endpoint, credentials) via the distribution's existing secret-handling mechanisms (environment variables and `_FILE`-suffixed secret resolution).\n46\t- Document the configuration path for pointing the vector I/O provider at an external, production-grade vector database — the migration path from the default store for users whose workloads outgrow it.\n47\t\n48\tOut of scope: provisioning the PostgreSQL instance itself (that is the downstream provisioning work selected by the go/no-go decision); benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824); any change to application storage.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- An OGX distribution deployment with the pgvector provider enabled serves vector insert, search, and retrieval operations over its vector I/O API against a remote PostgreSQL+pgvector instance, with the vector store running as a separate process from the OGX server (no inline provider in the serving path).\n53\t- The provider is activated by its documented environment guard, and the guard defaults leave non-RAG deployments unaffected.\n54\t- The application storage backend configuration is unchanged from its current defaults, and vector-store configuration can be set independently of it.\n55\t- Connection credentials are injectable via Kubernetes secrets using the distribution's existing secret-resolution mechanism, without exposing secrets in process arguments.\n56\t- A documented configuration path exists for switching the vector I/O provider to an external production-grade vector database (the user migration path off the default store).\n57\t- The playground's RAG vector operations work against the pgvector-backed provider with no loss of the current inline capability at the provider level.\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability\n60\t\n61\t- **HLR-2 (P0, provider-level share)** — Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability: this epic provides the provider capability; end-to-end playground validation is completed by the selected provisioning epic's acceptance criteria.\n62\t- **HLR-3 (P0)** — the default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance: delivered by the remote pgvector provider configuration.\n63\t- **HLR-6 (P2, bundled)** — migration path from the default vector store to a production-grade vector database: bundled here because it is documentation of the same provider-configuration surface this epic implements; splitting it out would double-touch the same configuration.\n64\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84927dd4c0085fae","agent_id":"af44f3bd2f5ac49aa","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"bb479b98-7229-42d8-a7b4-dbff0eeeb756","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:53:14.988Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-875ae26c89e8b519","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
