{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cb5ee1a528dba2b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed provisioning of the default pgvector instance\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: rewrite\n16\t  fallback_approach: If BFF-managed provisioning is rejected (lifecycle ownership\n17\t    or design concerns), fall back to operator-based self-provisioning once the dashboard\n18\t    operator modularization lands, or to bring-your-own PostgreSQL for the release.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 1\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 6\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\t---\n35\t# BFF-managed provisioning of the default pgvector instance\n36\t\n37\t> Conditional branch B — instantiated only if the provisioning go/no-go decision selects BFF-managed provisioning (the dashboard operator modularization does not land in time, and the team opts to preserve the out-of-the-box experience rather than requiring bring-your-own PostgreSQL).\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tThis epic implements out-of-the-box provisioning of the default vector store directly in the Gen AI Studio backend-for-frontend (the gen-ai BFF), as the alternative to an operator-based approach when the RHOAI-controlled operator layer is not available in the target release. Following the pattern the BFF already uses for guardrails infrastructure, the BFF creates the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance — Deployment, Service, persistent storage (PVC), and credentials Secret — when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, using the existing Red Hat `postgresql-16` image (pgvector included). The OGX instance is then wired to use it as its vector I/O provider.\n42\t\n43\tThe Staff Engineer Input flags the caveats that shape this epic: the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, and credential rotation need explicit design attention; and the implementation must be designed for portability, so that when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL becomes available (RHOAI 3.6) the BFF consumes the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n44\t\n45\t## Scope\n46\t\n47\t- Implement provisioning logic in the gen-ai BFF: when a playground with RAG is configured and no PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the pgvector-capable PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, and credentials Secret; reuse the existing instance when one is already present.\n48\t- Wire the provisioned instance into the OGX server configuration for the playground, activating the remote pgvector vector I/O provider with the provisioned connection details.\n49\t- Implement lifecycle handling explicitly (no reconciliation loop): cleanup of the database resources when the owning playground is deleted; recovery from BFF pod restarts (idempotent re-check of existing resources); a defined approach to credential rotation.\n50\t- Scope instances per namespace for multi-tenant isolation (per-namespace is the resolved scoping direction; the per-instance consumer, Auto Rag, is handled in RHAISTRAT-7824).\n51\t- Apply resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing, adjusted per the benchmark recommendation where available.\n52\t- Design for portability: prefer consuming an existing connection string when one is provided (the RHOAI 3.6 platform-shared database path) over always self-provisioning.\n53\t\n54\tOut of scope: the OGX-side provider enablement (delivered by the distribution's remote pgvector provider configuration); benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824); operator-based provisioning.\n55\t\n56\t## Acceptance Criteria\n57\t\n58\t- A user who creates a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG capabilities gets a working default vector store with no manual provisioning or external infrastructure configuration (out-of-the-box experience preserved).\n59\t- The PostgreSQL/pgvector Deployment, Service, PVC, and credentials Secret are created by the BFF only when absent in the namespace, and an existing suitable instance is reused rather than duplicated.\n60\t- Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function end-to-end against the default vector store with no loss of current capability (document ingestion, retrieval, and query flows).\n61\t- Deleting a playground cleans up the database resources it caused to be provisioned (or documents and implements the deliberate retention policy decided in design).\n62\t- A BFF pod restart does not leave orphaned or broken database resources: re-running the provisioning path is idempotent.\n63\t- Credential rotation is handled per the designed approach without breaking existing playground RAG workflows.\n64\t- Vector store instances are isolated per namespace; one namespace's playground cannot read another namespace's vector data through the default store.\n65\t- The provisioned instance's resource requests/limits follow the agreed sizing (model-registry-operator reference, adjusted per benchmark where applicable) and run without significant overhead in resource-constrained environments.\n66\t- The BFF consumes a provided shared connection string in preference to self-provisioning when one is available, demonstrating the RHOAI 3.6 portability path.\n67\t- The pgvector-capable PostgreSQL image reference used for provisioning is resolvable in disconnected installs (the related image env var already present in the rhods-operator CSV).\n68\t\n69\t## HLR Traceability\n70\t\n71\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — default vector store available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning: delivered by BFF-managed provisioning.\n72\t- **HLR-2 (P0)** — playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability: end-to-end validation acceptance criteria above.\n73\t- **HLR-4 (P1, bundled)** — multi-tenant scoping (per-namespace isolation): a property of the same provisioning surface; bundled rather than split.\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84927dd4c0085fae","agent_id":"af44f3bd2f5ac49aa","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"95944973-636d-4f0c-b16a-cf911100af71","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:53:19.477Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cb5ee1a528dba2b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
