r issues.\n39\t\n40\tThe decomposition was built without the full strategy attachment\n41\t(`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`, absent from this workspace) and says so\n42\texplicitly, deriving HLRs from the Acceptance Criteria and leaning on the\n43\tStaff Engineer Input, which takes precedence over the AI-generated Technical\n44\tApproach. All eight Staff Engineer corrections/overrides are logged and\n45\tfaithfully applied (no onboarding chain per Correction 2, Auto Rag descoped\n46\tper override 5 with HLR-7 recorded rather than silently dropped, vector vs\n47\tapplication storage kept independent per Correction 3, dev-experimentation\n48\tperformance scope per override 6).\n49\t\n50\t## Criterion Details\n51\t\n52\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n53\t\n54\tAll P0 HLRs (1, 2, 3) map to epics; HLR-7 (Auto Rag) is descoped per Staff\n55\tEngineer override 5 and recorded visibly in the matrix with its redirect to\n56\tRHAISTRAT-7824 — a staff-directed descope, not a coverage gap. P1 HLR-4 and\n57\tHLR-5 are covered; P2 HLR-6 is covered (bundled into E002 with recorded\n58\trationale in the matrix row). Priority inheritance is clean: E002 (P0) blocks\n59\tonly P0 branch work; E003 (P1) blocks nothing, and the summary's\n60\tpriority-inversion watch correctly explains why the benchmark epic is P1 on a\n61\tBlocker-priority strategy (it verifies rather than enables). Bundling of\n62\tHLR-4/HLR-6 into the same-surface P0 work is incidental polish on that work\n63\t(the per-namespace scoping is a property of the provisioning surface; the\n64\tmigration path is documentation of the same config surface), with the bundling\n65\trationale recorded in the matrix rows — not a priority collapse of deferrable\n66\tfeatures.\n67\t\n68\tMinor: the HLR-1 row lists \"RHAISTRAT-8004-C-E004\", missing the `BRANCH-`\n69\tprefix (should be `RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E004`).\n70\t\n71\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n72\t\n73\tNo circular dependencies; every diagram edge matches the target epic's\n74\tfrontmatter `dependencies` exactly, in both directions, with arrows drawn\n75\tdependency-to-dependent per the convention (E002→E003; E001→branches;\n76\tE002→branches). No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n77\tAll three branch files set both `branch` (matching filename) and `gated_by`\n78\t(E001, present in direct dependencies). Edges are justified: E002→E003\n79\t(benchmark runs against the provider-enabled build), E002→branches (the\n80\tprovisioning variants wire OGX instances against the provider surface E002\n81\tdelivers — a genuine artifact dependency, not a build-pipeline output consumed\n82\tas content), E001→branches (true gate — the outcome determines which epic\n83\texists, with `gate_failure_impact: rewrite` + fallback on every branch).\n84\tE001∥E002/E003 and E003∥branches are correctly unserialized; the sizing\n85\trecommendation is consumed as advisory content via AC (\"adjusted per benchmark\n86\twhere available\"), which is the rule-compliant shape. Critical path length 2\n87\tis reasonable — the strategy is genuinely parallel and a longer path would be\n88\tartificial serialization. No data/artifact dependency discoverable in epic\n89\tcontent is missing from the DAG.\n90\t\n91\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n92\t\n93\tComponent/team tuples are respected: E002 (OGX Core / OGX) is separate from\n94\tthe Gen AI Studio epics; the benchmark epic is separate from the provider\n95\tepic. Branch B's size is kept above the ~2-week guideline as one logical\n96\tchange WITH a concrete re-split trigger recorded in the Health Warning\n97\t(\"split the lifecycle-management work out if the design finds it needs\n98\treconciliation machinery beyond plain create/update/delete calls\") — a managed\n99\ttrade-off, within the guideline per the review rule. The three branch epics\n100\tare mutually exclusive alternatives, not scope duplicates. Component names are\n101\tcanonical (`Gen AI Studio`, `OGX Core` per `.context/rhai-components.txt`),\n