{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa90cf3233b2a298","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: Decide the PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning mechanism for the Gen AI Studio\n4\t  default vector store (go/no-go)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tdependencies: []\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -2\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Decide the PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning mechanism for the Gen AI Studio default vector store (go/no-go)\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves the single decision that determines which provisioning implementation exists for the Gen AI Studio default vector store: how the pgvector-backed PostgreSQL instance is provisioned for RHOAI 3.5. Three bounded outcomes were identified in the Staff Engineer Input:\n31\t\n32\t- **Operator-based self-provisioning** — viable only if the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164, In Progress) delivers a RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time; PostgreSQL lifecycle management then lives in that operator, following the model-registry-operator pattern.\n33\t- **BFF-managed provisioning** — the gen-ai backend-for-frontend provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly (Deployment, Service, persistent storage, credentials Secret) when a playground with RAG is configured and no instance exists in the namespace, mirroring how the BFF already provisions guardrails infrastructure.\n34\t- **Bring-your-own PostgreSQL** — users configure the OGXServer custom resource spec with their own PostgreSQL connection details; this is the deliberate fallback that aligns with the OGX 1.0 breaking changes.\n35\t\n36\tThe deliverable is a decision record containing the selection, the go/no-go criteria against RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing (the Staff Engineer Input directs that this gate be defined as part of sprint planning), and the supporting lifecycle and portability analysis for the selected mechanism.\n37\t\n38\t## Scope\n39\t\n40\t- Define the go/no-go gate: the concrete criteria and decision date by which RHAISTRAT-7164's delivery status selects (or rules out) operator-based self-provisioning for the target release.\n41\t- Analyze the lifecycle ownership of each option: creation, upgrade, cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart recovery, and credential rotation. For the BFF-managed option this analysis is mandatory — the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so cleanup, restart handling, and credential rotation need explicit design answers before that option can be selected.\n42\t- Analyze portability: the selected mechanism must be designed so that, when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), Gen AI Studio consumes a shared connection string rather than self-provisioning.\n43\t- Confirm the resource-sizing starting point (the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits) applies to the selected mechanism.\n44\t- Record the decision and rationale in a decision record that the downstream provisioning implementation can be planned against.\n45\t\n46\tOut of scope: implementing any provisioning mechanism; the OGX-side pgvector provider configuration; benchmarking; Auto Rag compatibility (RHAISTRAT-7824).\n47\t\n48\t## Acceptance Criteria\n49\t\n50\t- A decision record exists that selects exactly one of: operator-based self-provisioning, BFF-managed provisioning, or bring-your-own PostgreSQL for the default vector store in the target release.\n51\t- The decision record states the go/no-go criteria and decision point against RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery timing, so the gate can be re-run mechanically in sprint planning.\n52\t- The decision record documents lifecycle ownership (creation, upgrade, cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart recovery, credential rotation) for the selected mechanism, including how the BFF-managed option addresses each lifecycle concern if selected.\n53\t- The decision record documents how the selected mechanism remains portable to the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided shared PostgreSQL (consuming a connection string rather than self-provisioning).\n54\t- The decision record identifies which downstream provisioning work the selection instantiates, in sufficient detail for that work to be scheduled.\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability\n57\t\n58\t- **HLR-1 (P0)** — a default vector store available out-of-the-box: this investigation determines which mechanism delivers (or explicitly defers, in the bring-your-own outcome) that requirement, and is the prerequisite for all provisioning implementation work.\n59\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-adf5699511f21d55","agent_id":"a391236d18796d006","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"440b5a25-20e2-45a7-9480-b72a0c5627be","timestamp":"2026-10-26T09:01:43.510Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa90cf3233b2a298","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
