{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a502c67b4bfe2a3a","type":"tool_result","content":"skills/epic-decompose/prompts/revise-agent.md\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md\n----\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/revise-agent.md:32:- **Rule 11 (docs-authoring not blocked by an Implementation epic)**: add the missing epic to the docs epic's `dependencies` (and to the summary's DAG diagram + justification).\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/revise-agent.md:63:  - `docs-authoring` epic → \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:48:- Rule 11 (docs-authoring epic not blocked by an Implementation epic) → severity critical, criterion \"DAG Coherence\" (missing edge)\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:70:Check: Read the strategy's HLR list. For each HLR, verify it appears in at least one epic's \"HLR Traceability\" section. Verify priority inheritance — an epic blocking all P0 work must be P0. Exception: `docs-authoring` epics are exempt from priority inheritance; their priority derives from the strategy's Jira priority (Critical→P0, Major→P1, Normal/Minor/Undefined→P2), not from the implementations they depend on. Do not flag a `docs-authoring` epic's dependency on a lower-priority implementation as a priority inheritance violation. Check for priority collapse — if an epic maps to HLRs at multiple priority levels and the lower-priority HLRs are distinct deferrable features (not incidental polish on the P0 work), they should be in separate epics so they can be planned independently. Priority collapse with deferrable features is a major issue — the ability to defer work independently is a planning requirement that overrides boundary convenience. **Exception: triaged strategies.** If `triage: below-threshold` or `triage: docs-only`, the single-epic output is correct by design (Step 0 takes precedence over priority-split logic). Do not flag priority collapse on a triaged strategy — all HLRs are bundled intentionally.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:78:Check: Trace the dependency graph. Verify each edge against the DAG construction rules (boundary rules 1-3, investigation edges 4-5, implementation type ordering 6-12, implementation edges 13-16, external dependency edges 17-19, generation rules 20-23, AC rules 24-26). Check that parallel-eligible work (different repos, no shared artifacts) is not unnecessarily serialized. **Implementation-type parallelism (rules 7, 8, 10) — enumerate and check every pair:** epics whose `implementation_type` pairing is mandated parallel must NOT have a blocking edge between them in either direction. Check **every** `konflux-onboarding` epic against **every** general-implementation epic (a decomposition often has more than one general epic — e.g. a component controller AND an OLM/CSV packaging epic; check all of them, not just the most obvious one). Specifically, a `konflux-onboarding → general implementation` edge (rule 10), `license-validation → repo-onboarding` (rule 7), or `license-validation → konflux-onboarding` (rule 8) is a **critical** issue — score this criterion 0. The rule covers ANY general implementation epic, whatever its component or deliverable — including stack-wide assembly, packaging, or evidence epics that consume the pipeline's outputs; \"the dependent epic is not the onboarded component's own implementation\" is not an exemption. The only permitted coupling between a `konflux-onboarding` epic and a general implementation epic is the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion (or an equivalent AC on the consuming epic), never a `dependencies`/DAG edge. Do not accept a rationale that the code \"needs the pipeline first.\" **In particular, an edge justified only by an image digest / SHA / `RELATED_IMAGE` / endpoint URL — or by any other build-pipeline output consumed as CONTENT (signed attestations, SBOMs, provenance, scan results) — is a configuration reference (Rule 13), which must be an AC gate, not a `dependencies` edge — flag it as critical, do not accept it as an \"artifact dependency.\"** Symmetrically, do not report the absence of such an edge as a missing data/artifact dependency in the cross-check below: a build-pipeline output consumed as content is an AC gate, and its absence from the DAG is the rule-compliant shape. **Conditional branch files (`{ID}-BRANCH-<letter>-E*.md`), when present:** each MUST set both `branch` (the outcome letter, matching the filename) and `gated_by` (the main-plan Investigation `epic_id` that selects it) — a branch file missing either is a **critical** issue because the pipeline silently drops it. And no main-plan epic may list a branch epic in its `dependencies`: a main-plan epic depending on a `-BRANCH-` node (e.g. main-plan `E004` → `BRANCH-A-E003`) references an epic that is never created as an issue, a **critical** issue — work that hinges on the outcome must depend on the Investigation epic itself, and work that hinges on a specific branch belongs inside that branch. Note: Rule 11 edges (all implementations → `docs-authoring`) are valid DAG edges but do not trigger priority inheritance — do not flag them as unjustified serialization or priority inheritance violations. Verify critical path length against strategy size heuristics (S: 1-2, M standard: 3-4, M with new component: 4-5, L: 5-7). **Cross-check consistency**: The DAG diagram convention is `graph TD` with arrows from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` means \"E001 must complete before E003 can start\"). Verify that every edge in the decomposition summary DAG diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa. If arrows are drawn in the opposite direction (dependent → dependency), flag as a critical issue — the diagram is misleading even if frontmatter is correct. Any mismatch (edge in diagram but not in frontmatter, or frontmatter dependency referencing a nonexistent epic) is a critical issue — score 0. **Cross-check completeness**: also scan epic content (scope, ACs, descriptions) for data/artifact dependencies not captured in the DAG — e.g., an epic that consumes a schema, image, or API produced by another epic but has no edge to it. A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue even if the diagram and frontmatter are consistent with each other.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:108:- **2**: Each epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: rollback/feature-flag for replacements, doc review for docs-authoring, build pipeline green for konflux chain.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:112:Check: Verify each epic has ACs. Check that replacement epics have rollback/feature-flag ACs, docs-authoring has technical review AC, and konflux-chain epics have build pipeline AC. Any missing rule-mandated AC is a major issue — this applies to every epic that meets the rule's criteria, not just the first or most obvious one. Also scan every AC for non-testable entries: an AC that states an externally-controlled precondition — a schedule constraint (lab turnaround, review-board cadence, vendor timing, \"cannot be compressed by engineering effort\") or an availability precondition granted by parties outside the strategy (procurement, dedicated hardware/node allocation, partner-lab access, e.g. \"dedicated hardware nodes are available to the campaign\") — is a risk note misfiled as an AC (the canonical soft-AC example); it belongs in the epic's Risks/Notes or the summary's Health Warnings. Do not accept \"the epic's work includes securing the access\" as a defense: securing access is work, but the access being granted is controlled by others — the AC belongs on the epic's own deliverable. A condition on what the epic itself builds or verifies (e.g. \"the runtime this epic produces supports the cleared model set\") is a valid AC even if it uses the word \"available\". Flag each misfiled AC as an issue (severity minor) with the correction \"move to Risks/Notes\". Also check that epic bodies do not reference sibling epics by draft ID (e.g., \"E001\", \"E003\") — draft IDs are meaningless in Jira. Each reference is a minor issue; 3+ across the decomposition costs a point.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:120:Check: Compare the strategy's scope, acceptance criteria, and capabilities against the combined epic set. Look for gaps. Also check cross-epic consistency: when an upstream epic's scope covers multiple items (modules, components, APIs), verify that the downstream epic set collectively accounts for all of them — not silently dropped. Verify that strategy-level context relevant to an epic's scope (risks, assumptions, open questions, stakeholder commitments, etc.) is carried forward — not silently dropped. **Docs-authoring check**: if the strategy's Prerequisites & Process Gates section lists `Documentation Support: Yes`, the epic set must include a `docs-authoring` epic (Rule 11). A missing docs-authoring epic when documentation support is required is a major issue — score 0.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:22:**Check 2 — Documentation only**: If all affected components have \"No code changes\" or \"reference only\": produce a single epic file with `implementation_type: docs-authoring`, content outline, and mandatory accuracy validation against architecture context. Write the decomposition summary. Then stop.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:104:- `docs-authoring` priority exception: `docs-authoring` epics are exempt from priority inheritance. They do not push their priority upstream to the implementations they depend on. Instead, derive their priority from the strategy frontmatter `priority` field: Critical→P0, Major→P1, Normal/Minor/Undefined→P2. If the field is missing or empty, default to P2.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:114:   - **Work-product type boundaries**: code implementation, content authoring (sample notebooks, tutorials, curated examples), and documentation are different kinds of work — they require different skills, different review criteria, and produce different PR review cycles. An epic that mixes code implementation with content authoring should be split even if the same team owns both. **Exception — incidental documentation touch-ups:** updating captions, screenshots, or references that fall out of a code change (no new content authored) is not a separate work stream — fold it into the originating epic as scope/acceptance criteria rather than a standalone `docs-authoring` epic. Only substantive content/documentation authoring (new tutorials, sample notebooks, curated examples, new or rewritten doc topics), or a strategy with `Documentation Support: Yes`, warrants a separate epic.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:128:11. `docs-authoring` blocked by ALL Implementation epics in strategy (always last; docs describe what was built). These edges do not trigger priority inheritance — see Step 4 `docs-authoring` priority exception.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:149:24. `docs-authoring` epic → add \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:174:| `docs-authoring` | Red Hat docs content process — specialized tooling |\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:299:   The four section headings are literal and required for EVERY epic, including `docs-authoring` epics — an outline of content topics goes INSIDE the `## Scope` section, never as a renamed `## Content Outline` heading (a renamed heading fails the artifact contract).\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/decompose-agent.md:357:It deterministically checks what downstream consumers enforce and an LLM step can silently get wrong: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), `component` values verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by every Implementation epic), priority inheritance (an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0), implementation-type parallelism (Rules 7/8/10 — no `dependencies` edge, in either direction, between `license-validation` and `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation` and `konflux-onboarding`, or `konflux-onboarding` and a general implementation; a flagged edge is removed from the dependent epic's `dependencies` and its coupling expressed as an AC gate instead), and the two summary-side recording rules: an epic kept above the ~2-week guideline must carry a re-split trigger (check 6), and a lower-priority HLR bundled into a higher-priority epic must be flagged `bundled` with its rationale in the HLR Traceability Matrix row (check 7). It prints one `VIOLATION:` line per problem (each names the exact fix) and exits non-zero. Fix every violation by editing the epic files **or the decomposition summary** (never edit the script), re-run, and proceed only once it prints `OK`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91d83d54e068c69a","agent_id":"af523506aa080761a","session_id":"6c664dc7-c756-4423-855a-2ea0889c4084","uuid":"ba6ed552-fd25-4206-8421-35f16bf6b1a7","timestamp":"2026-10-26T09:05:45.206Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a502c67b4bfe2a3a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
